WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.630
Speaker 1: Let's go to Colin from Ukraine.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.790
Are you in Ukraine, Kellan?

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.510
Speaker 6: Hi, David, can you hear me?

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:11.270
Yes, I can.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:18.280
I live in Kiev and I've lived there for the
last 11 years, but I actually came back to

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.830
the U.S. for business on February 1st.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.130
Oh, OK.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:28.160
But my wife, my cat and all of her family
are still in Ukraine, and she actually just

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.579
got out of Kiev with an armed guard last week
and went to the West.

00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:34.579
Speaker 1: Wow.

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:35.710
And do you like so OK?

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.579
Talk me through the logistics of that.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:42.219
Do you hire an armed guard to help your wife
get to the west of the country?

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:46.729
Speaker 6: So her dad is fairly well connected.

00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:52.489
He is a director of a regional hospital in
western Ukraine.

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:55.239
So he has a lot of people who owe him favors.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:56.239
I see.

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:01.940
And so he was able to talk to one of his connections
who happened to be in Kiev at the time of

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.860
the invasion, and he had special military
permission to be out after curfew.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.550
And he was also allowed to carry a concealed
weapon.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:17.500
So he came to our apartment and took our car
with my wife and cat and some of her belongings.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.920
Speaker 1: So the cat went with your wife.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.660
That's good to hear, at least.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.660
Yeah.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.710
Now let me ask you this.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.750
Have you have you have been not you, but have
you together as a unit?

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.910
Are you planning to go back to Kiev when?

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:40.300
Speaker 6: As soon as the proper number of
Russians have been sent home in body bags.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.300
Wow.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.960
Speaker 1: So in other words, you're not abandoning
Kiev, you're just waiting for the violence

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:45.960
to end.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.410
Speaker 6: Yeah, and I left there on February
1st for business purposes to come back to

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.020
the U.S.. Yeah.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.480
And was was planning on returning.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.190
But it's not.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.190
It's not possible.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:03.470
I mean, it's very hard to even get back into
Kiev right now if you want to, unless you

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.310
have some kind of military protection.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:13.450
And I'm not a trained soldier, I and the Ukrainian
government has asked weekend warriors to stay

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.510
away.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:15.970
And that's exactly what I would be.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.099
So I'm respecting that that call from the
government.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.379
Speaker 1: And are you American married to
a Ukrainian woman or are you Ukrainian?

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:24.860
Speaker 6: I'm American.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.250
Married to Ukrainian.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.579
I have residency in the country.

00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:30.739
OK, yeah.

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:33.069
We have a couple of apartments in Kiev.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:40.239
A rocket that was fired into the city and
landed near our bank, which is about two blocks

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:46.730
away from where our home and destroyed quite
a bit of it, knocked out all the windows and

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:47.730
the surrounding area.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.730
So.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.760
Speaker 1: So can you talk a little bit about,
you know, in the news we see Russia has has

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:59.019
bombed random apartment buildings and then
later you'll hear the attempt at a rationalization

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:05.370
by saying, Well, we believe dissidents and
combatants were planning violent activities

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.930
in those apartment buildings.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.060
Do you know anything about that?

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.689
Is it literally just like all of a sudden,
just a missile hits an apartment building?

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:17.849
Speaker 6: So the Territorial Defense Forces
are set up basically in every district in

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:19.159
the city.

00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:26.319
So there are people who have weapons on the
ground in quite a few places in the city.

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:33.639
But like I just saw the destruction around
Lukyanov Lukyanov metro station, which is

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:36.389
about 10 minutes away from our apartment.

00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:37.389
Yeah.

00:03:37.389 --> 00:03:44.370
And I didn't see any military on the ground
near that metro station, but it wouldn't surprise

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:51.169
me if there were territorial defense forces
in and around that area because it is a major

00:03:51.169 --> 00:03:52.999
metro station.

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:53.999
It's fairly deep.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:59.609
Speaker 1: So in a literal sense, when Russia
says, well, those apartment buildings in or

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:02.559
right outside of them had forces.

00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:09.999
So Ukrainian forces
Speaker 6: every room of every building in

00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:13.969
Ukraine right now as people that are willing
to kill Russians in it.

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:15.319
Speaker 1: Right, right, right, right.

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:16.319
So it just sort of.

00:04:16.319 --> 00:04:17.319
Yeah.

00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:18.319
Wow, wow.

00:04:18.319 --> 00:04:19.319
All right.

00:04:19.319 --> 00:04:20.690
And then so and for the time being, you're
just hanging tight in the US.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.720
Speaker 6: Yeah, I'm actually I'm in Austin
with my younger brother.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.700
And, you know, still trying to go about business
life is as much as I can.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:36.720
I've been doing quite a few interviews with
with local media back in my home state of

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.410
Nebraska, just trying to get the word out.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.060
And this was actually part of my what.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:50.770
What I wanted to ask you about is I have firsthand
experience of living in that country for 11

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:59.670
years, getting to know thousands of Ukrainians
and never seeing any kind of neo-Nazi movements

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:05.910
or any other than the very far right which,
like the Azov Battalion, does have a very

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.160
right wing leanings.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.780
And there are some people, but not any more
so than than there are in any other country

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:13.780
in the world.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:19.980
I mean, their Jewish president got, I think
it was 71 or 73 percent of the vote.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:27.470
So I've talked to a lot of people in the U.S.
who are just repeating ad nauseum the same

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:33.470
Russian propaganda that I got to hear aplenty
from Russian media.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:37.240
Speaker 1: And are the people repeating that
stuff in the U.S. and your experience?

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.090
Do they tend to be more on the right or the
left in the U.S.?

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.300
Speaker 6: Tinfoil hat right wingers?

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.220
Oh, OK, OK, OK.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:52.190
Well, my question is if I can't convince them,
having lived in the country for 11 years,

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:57.220
participated in the Maidan revolution on the
ground.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.970
And I actually have firsthand knowledge of
this kind of stuff.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:09.230
What hope is there for any kind of intelligent
conversation about when we should put boots

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:13.880
on the ground, when we should go in there,
how much protection we should provide them,

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.830
why we should care about them if I can't convince
them of that.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:20.070
What hope is there of anyone else to convince
them?

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.070
Speaker 1: No.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:24.870
Regardless of your views as to what the answers
to those questions should be with the people

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.680
you're describing, you're not going to be
able to have a real, sensible conversation

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.450
about those issues, sadly, but you're just
not.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.350
All right
Speaker 6: now.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:35.060
Well, thanks for taking my call and for listening.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.570
Speaker 1: All right, well, listen, let's
hope everybody in the family and everybody

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.420
in Ukraine stays safe, OK?

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:41.420
All right.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:42.420
All right.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.350
There is Kellen, who lives in Ukraine.

